WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.087
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Provider Notices.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:10.438
This is Chapter 6: routing to
owners, and Ticket-to-Machine tasks.

4
00:00:10.438 --> 00:00:13.534
A notice nobody owns is
a notice nobody reads,

5
00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:17.300
so routing gives each one an
owner once, and never twice.

6
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.284
We'll start from the Ticket-to-Machine
pattern and its finding contract,

7
00:00:21.284 --> 00:00:25.403
see what they leave to you for provider
notices, then read the routing file,

8
00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:29.725
the task payload and the state that makes
the router de-duplicate across runs.

9
00:00:29.725 --> 00:00:33.075
Then two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

10
00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:34.650
Two notes before we begin.

11
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.012
This chapter is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

12
00:00:38.012 --> 00:00:41.890
And the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

13
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:46.628
Why route at all?

14
00:00:46.628 --> 00:00:50.240
Because a notice nobody owns
is a notice nobody reads.

15
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.682
The collectors in Chapters 1 to 5
fill one record for every provider;

16
00:00:54.682 --> 00:00:57.934
this chapter turns the
ones that need action into

17
00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:01.027
work with a named owner,
and does it exactly once.

18
00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:05.866
By the end you can write and check a routing
file, route a collection as a dry run,

19
00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:07.907
read every payload it would send,

20
00:01:07.907 --> 00:01:11.233
and explain two behaviors
that matter in production:

21
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:14.786
why a second run writes nothing,
and why a task closes only

22
00:01:14.786 --> 00:01:18.840
when the agency re-reads, not
when the provider says it's done.

23
00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.215
Here's where notices go today.

24
00:01:24.215 --> 00:01:29.258
Compliance Automation and Day-2 Operations
routes Microsoft's service health email

25
00:01:29.258 --> 00:01:30.892
to the help desk supervisor,

26
00:01:30.892 --> 00:01:34.727
and it's clear that nothing is
approved on the strength of an email.

27
00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:37.978
There's no owner, no
due date and no closure.

28
00:01:37.978 --> 00:01:42.537
With the router, every provider,
Azure, Microsoft 365 in GCC,

29
00:01:42.537 --> 00:01:46.527
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,
AWS and Google Cloud,

30
00:01:46.527 --> 00:01:52.416
arrives as the same record, and one
routing file decides what becomes a task.

31
00:01:52.416 --> 00:01:54.915
Notice that routing calls no cloud at all.

32
00:01:54.915 --> 00:01:56.578
It adds no provider fact;

33
00:01:56.578 --> 00:02:01.020
it writes down the agency's own
decision about who owns what.

34
00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.058
The router implements a pattern
the workbook already has.

35
00:02:08.058 --> 00:02:11.749
Ticket-to-Machine, from
Governance, Drift and Evidence,

36
00:02:11.749 --> 00:02:15.278
turns a finding into a typed
task with an owner, a lane,

37
00:02:15.278 --> 00:02:19.791
and a request chain of five clauses:
where it came from, who approves,

38
00:02:19.791 --> 00:02:24.632
what to do, the re-read check that proves
it was done, and the evidence kept.

39
00:02:24.632 --> 00:02:29.393
Its finding contract sets four
severities and their response windows:

40
00:02:29.393 --> 00:02:33.346
immediate, one hour, twenty-four
hours and seventy-two hours.

41
00:02:33.346 --> 00:02:35.395
Be clear about what those windows are.

42
00:02:35.395 --> 00:02:38.930
They're the program's design,
not a federal requirement.

43
00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.068
What does the pattern
leave to you for notices?

44
00:02:45.068 --> 00:02:48.222
A provider's own deadline is
a different date from the

45
00:02:48.222 --> 00:02:51.305
agency's response window, so
the kit keeps them apart.

46
00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:53.961
A provider repeats the
same notice every week,

47
00:02:53.961 --> 00:02:58.643
so the kit keeps state with fingerprints,
and an unchanged notice writes nothing.

48
00:02:58.643 --> 00:03:02.781
A provider closing its notice
isn't the agency's work being done,

49
00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:04.692
so a close asks for the re-read.

50
00:03:04.692 --> 00:03:09.380
Five providers and six kinds mean many
owners, so there's a routing file.

51
00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.917
And a retirement that names your
resources is a contingency plan change.

52
00:03:13.917 --> 00:03:18.310
The verdict: keep the contract
exactly, and build the rest.

53
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.248
The routing is a JSON file
the agency writes at work,

54
00:03:25.248 --> 00:03:29.596
from the kit's template or from a
worksheet with the kit's Copilot prompts,

55
00:03:29.596 --> 00:03:32.060
and it stays inside the agency's boundary.

56
00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.660
The lab's copy uses fictional teams.

57
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:40.435
Each rule has an ID, a match, and
a route: task, digest or ignore.

58
00:03:40.435 --> 00:03:43.519
A task rule adds a severity,
an assignment group,

59
00:03:43.519 --> 00:03:47.472
an approver role and whether a
contingency review is needed.

60
00:03:47.472 --> 00:03:51.563
Matches can be on provider, kind
and status, on whether action is

61
00:03:51.563 --> 00:03:56.047
required or resources are named, or
a pattern on services and regions.

62
00:03:56.047 --> 00:03:59.130
And the file is checked
before anything runs.

63
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.430
Order matters, because rules are tried
in order and the first match wins.

64
00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:11.176
The workbook's design guidance is
to put security advisories and

65
00:04:11.176 --> 00:04:15.130
retirements before the general rule
for anything that needs action.

66
00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:20.006
Here are the lab's six rules: security
to a security operations team at P2,

67
00:04:20.006 --> 00:04:24.552
retirements and maintenance to
cloud platform engineering at P3,

68
00:04:24.552 --> 00:04:28.932
incidents to the service desk at
P2, changes to cloud operations,

69
00:04:28.932 --> 00:04:32.155
and one rule that ignores
a Microsoft Viva post.

70
00:04:32.155 --> 00:04:35.019
Get the order wrong,
with a broad rule first,

71
00:04:35.019 --> 00:04:38.820
and security advisories
land in the general queue.

72
00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.526
For each notice that needs
a task, the kit writes one

73
00:04:45.526 --> 00:04:49.045
ServiceNow-shaped payload, and
checks it before it's written.

74
00:04:49.045 --> 00:04:52.480
It says whether this is a
create, an update or a close,

75
00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.733
and carries a de-duplication key
made of the provider and its ID.

76
00:04:56.733 --> 00:05:00.835
It has the severity, priority,
response window and assignment group,

77
00:05:00.835 --> 00:05:03.692
a description with
everything the owner needs,

78
00:05:03.692 --> 00:05:08.233
and the Ticket-to-Machine part: the
human decision lane and the five clauses.

79
00:05:08.233 --> 00:05:10.095
Look closely at the two dates.

80
00:05:10.095 --> 00:05:13.657
The response due date is the
agency's, from the severity.

81
00:05:13.657 --> 00:05:16.119
The action due date is the provider's.

82
00:05:16.119 --> 00:05:19.760
Mix them up, and a routine
task looks due in a year.

83
00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:26.310
Each run compares every
notice with the state.

84
00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.348
A new open notice gets a create.

85
00:05:28.348 --> 00:05:32.861
A changed one, with a different fingerprint
or a changed rule, gets an update.

86
00:05:32.861 --> 00:05:37.260
When the provider closes it, the task
gets a close that asks for the re-read.

87
00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.901
And if an open notice simply disappears
from a provider the kit read this run,

88
00:05:41.901 --> 00:05:46.248
it's marked absent, and its task gets
an update asking for the same re-read.

89
00:05:46.248 --> 00:05:49.436
A provider that wasn't read
is never marked absent.

90
00:05:49.436 --> 00:05:51.473
Unchanged notices write nothing.

91
00:05:51.473 --> 00:05:54.978
And the whole run is a dry run:
nothing is sent unless the

92
00:05:54.978 --> 00:05:58.850
scheduled run is told to send
and the settings allow it.

93
00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:03.650
Who's involved?

94
00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.632
The change manager owns this gate,

95
00:06:05.632 --> 00:06:09.738
and reviews the routing file and the
change records the payloads open.

96
00:06:09.738 --> 00:06:14.251
The security officer reviews the security
route and keeps the run folders.

97
00:06:14.251 --> 00:06:17.562
In the lab's payload, the
system owner is the approver,

98
00:06:17.562 --> 00:06:19.950
and owns the contingency plan review.

99
00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:24.810
And someone builds the ServiceNow side:
the endpoint that accepts the payload,

100
00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.100
whose shape is the agency's
and not yet specified here.

101
00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.449
One rule about where the routing lives:

102
00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:33.900
it names owners and assignment
groups, so it stays at work.

103
00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:38.730
The public repository ships only
templates and fictional examples.

104
00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.265
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, in the kit's folder,

105
00:06:46.265 --> 00:06:49.630
against fictional notices
and fictional teams.

106
00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:50.805
Nothing is sent.

107
00:06:50.805 --> 00:06:54.318
The finding contract the router
mirrors comes in the same download.

108
00:06:54.318 --> 00:06:58.923
Step 1 checks the lab's routing: six
rules, in the order they're tried,

109
00:06:58.923 --> 00:07:02.505
each with its route, severity
and assignment group.

110
00:07:02.505 --> 00:07:05.614
Step 2 tries the template
exactly as it ships,

111
00:07:05.614 --> 00:07:08.555
and it's refused: it
still has a placeholder.

112
00:07:08.555 --> 00:07:09.705
That's deliberate.

113
00:07:09.705 --> 00:07:14.050
The template can't run until every
placeholder is filled in at work.

114
00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:22.194
Step 3 collects every provider
from the day-one fixtures:

115
00:07:22.194 --> 00:07:27.336
twenty-two notices from five providers,
Azure, Microsoft 365 in GCC,

116
00:07:27.336 --> 00:07:33.600
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure, AWS and Google
Cloud, each valid against the schema.

117
00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.062
Step 4 routes them as a dry run.

118
00:07:36.062 --> 00:07:37.487
All twenty-two are new.

119
00:07:37.487 --> 00:07:41.177
Twelve route to a task,
nine go to the digest only,

120
00:07:41.177 --> 00:07:44.488
and one is ignored, the
Microsoft Viva post.

121
00:07:44.488 --> 00:07:48.621
So the run writes twelve create
payloads, each with its severity

122
00:07:48.621 --> 00:07:53.040
and assignment group, and the last
line says it: nothing was sent.

123
00:07:56.790 --> 00:08:01.077
Step 5 reads one payload: the
Azure retirement from Chapter 1.

124
00:08:01.077 --> 00:08:03.878
It's a create, with
the de-duplication key.

125
00:08:03.878 --> 00:08:06.912
It's P3, so the response
window is twenty-four

126
00:08:06.912 --> 00:08:09.778
hours and the response
is due the next day.

127
00:08:09.778 --> 00:08:15.439
But the provider's action date is
September 30, 2027, kept in its own field.

128
00:08:15.439 --> 00:08:20.009
It's routed to cloud platform engineering,
it asks for a contingency review,

129
00:08:20.009 --> 00:08:23.252
and it names the controls
and indicators it supports.

130
00:08:23.252 --> 00:08:27.578
Then the five clauses: the human
decision lane, where it came from,

131
00:08:27.578 --> 00:08:33.160
the system owner as approver, what to do,
the re-read check, and the evidence kept.

132
00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:40.972
Step 6 routes the same collection
again, on a copy of the state.

133
00:08:40.972 --> 00:08:44.653
All twenty-two are unchanged,
so nothing is written:

134
00:08:44.653 --> 00:08:48.685
no payloads, because nothing
new or changed needs a task.

135
00:08:48.685 --> 00:08:50.548
That's what keeps the queue readable.

136
00:08:50.548 --> 00:08:53.486
Step 7 collects and
routes the second week.

137
00:08:53.486 --> 00:08:59.448
Two notices are new, six changed, fifteen
unchanged, and one is no longer listed.

138
00:08:59.448 --> 00:09:05.272
The router writes just six payloads:
one create, four updates and one close.

139
00:09:05.272 --> 00:09:09.391
The routing hasn't changed, so the
same twelve notices route to tasks,

140
00:09:09.391 --> 00:09:13.140
but only the six with something
new to say make a payload.

141
00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:20.115
Step 8 reads what each of
the six payloads is for.

142
00:09:20.115 --> 00:09:25.350
Two rescheduled maintenance windows,
one Azure and one Oracle Cloud,

143
00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:28.840
and an updated Microsoft
365 post are updates.

144
00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:31.888
The Azure incident the
provider no longer lists

145
00:09:31.888 --> 00:09:34.478
is an absent update,
asking for the re-read.

146
00:09:34.478 --> 00:09:38.352
The new Microsoft 365 post is a create.

147
00:09:38.352 --> 00:09:42.861
And the AWS security notification
that AWS closed is a close,

148
00:09:42.861 --> 00:09:45.115
which also asks for the re-read.

149
00:09:45.115 --> 00:09:48.635
Step 9 runs the router's
tests, including the one that

150
00:09:48.635 --> 00:09:52.565
reads the finding contract:
thirty-one passed, none failed.

151
00:09:52.565 --> 00:09:54.770
Then delete the output folder.

152
00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:02.973
Lab 2 sends one payload to a ServiceNow
personal developer instance or a

153
00:10:02.973 --> 00:10:08.020
sub-production instance, never production,
with an endpoint you build to accept it.

154
00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:11.620
Its shape is the agency's,
and not yet specified here.

155
00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:14.519
Put a token for a test
integration user in the

156
00:10:14.519 --> 00:10:17.732
environment for this session
only, never in a file.

157
00:10:17.732 --> 00:10:21.613
Then send the Azure retirement
payload through the kit's sender,

158
00:10:21.613 --> 00:10:24.982
which posts it as JSON with
the token as a bearer token.

159
00:10:24.982 --> 00:10:27.780
Open the record and check
the assignment group,

160
00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.794
the priority, the description
and the five clauses.

161
00:10:30.794 --> 00:10:34.890
Then delete the record, clear
the token and delete the output.

162
00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.352
Here's what the routing proves,
against NIST's control catalog.

163
00:10:42.352 --> 00:10:45.474
A routing file checked before
it runs supports reviewing

164
00:10:45.474 --> 00:10:48.465
proposed changes under
configuration change control.

165
00:10:48.465 --> 00:10:53.445
A task with an owner, a severity and a
window for every notice that needs action

166
00:10:53.445 --> 00:10:58.815
supports disseminating security advisories
and acting on them in set time frames.

167
00:10:58.815 --> 00:11:03.089
Reviewing a retirement before it
lands also supports impact analysis,

168
00:11:03.089 --> 00:11:07.653
and the contingency review supports
keeping the contingency plan current.

169
00:11:07.653 --> 00:11:10.225
The key security indicators are FedRAMP's,

170
00:11:10.225 --> 00:11:13.165
and the mapping is the
program's design guidance.

171
00:11:13.165 --> 00:11:18.200
The kit's records support these controls;
the agency's assessor decides them.

172
00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:23.350
What breaks in production?

173
00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:27.740
The biggest one: a task resolved because
the provider resolved its notice,

174
00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:30.762
while the resource still
runs the retired product.

175
00:11:30.762 --> 00:11:35.663
That's why a close and an absent notice
both ask for the agency's own re-read.

176
00:11:35.663 --> 00:11:39.499
Ticket the same notice every week
and people stop reading the queue.

177
00:11:39.499 --> 00:11:44.200
Put a broad rule first and security
advisories land in the general queue.

178
00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.037
Publish the routing and
you publish who owns what.

179
00:11:47.037 --> 00:11:51.487
Put a token in the settings file and a
secret gets filed with the change record.

180
00:11:51.487 --> 00:11:55.004
Confuse the two dates, or let
the finding contract drift,

181
00:11:55.004 --> 00:11:57.820
and priorities stop meaning anything.

182
00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:02.920
Here's what done looks like.

183
00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:07.393
The agency's routing was written at work
from the template, passes the kit's check,

184
00:12:07.393 --> 00:12:09.562
and was reviewed by the change manager,

185
00:12:09.562 --> 00:12:12.408
and by the security officer
for the security route.

186
00:12:12.408 --> 00:12:16.707
Security advisories and retirements
route before the general rule.

187
00:12:16.707 --> 00:12:20.033
Someone read a dry run's
payloads before any were sent.

188
00:12:20.033 --> 00:12:22.820
A second run on the same
collection writes nothing.

189
00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:26.733
Tasks are resolved on the re-read,
not on the provider's closure.

190
00:12:26.733 --> 00:12:30.995
And the token lives only in the
environment of the run that sends.

191
00:12:30.995 --> 00:12:36.633
Next, Chapter 7 writes the weekly digest
and records every run as evidence.

192
00:12:36.633 --> 00:12:37.780
Thanks for watching.
